+# Consolidated final fix wave
+
+Base: `f72f858`. Authority: the three Important findings in `final-review.md`, the director’s-cut brief, and the controller’s explicit bounded final-fix assignment. Worker: Codex implementer. No agents spawned, source photo/MP4/logo edits, push, merge, deploy, full build or full suite performed by this worker. The controller built the current candidate, owns final full gates and regenerated canonical captures/metrics, and will obtain the scoped re-review.
+
+## 1. Source-frame fades for rear and white photographs
+
+The generic picture reset left these two images contained inside full-screen frames, so masks around the screen could not dissolve their actual edges. Both now have centered frames with their source aspect ratios (650/1050 and 470/805), mobile width constraints, crossed horizontal/vertical feather masks, and restrained local top/side shading. Their original pixels, helmets/#17 and individual timeline intervals are unchanged. No body cutout, generated subject detail or photo-byte edit was made.
+
+The new source-fixture regression first failed because the rear frame occupied all 1440px of the desktop viewport. After the CSS repair, desktop and mobile bounds/aspect/mask/shading assertions passed in managed Chromium and WebKit. Actual built-page captures include desktop 1440×900 and touch-enabled managed WebKit 390×844 rear/white midpoints and holds. Files and geometry/mask/shade/scroll evidence are in `/tmp/directors-final-wave-visual/`, with eight screenshots and `validation.json`. Inspected the four hold images: the exposed hard source rectangles are feathered, top lettering/backgrounds subdued, and helmet/#17 remain visible. Residual source field/background detail remains, deliberately rather than reconstructing the subject.
+
+## 2. Native WebKit keyboard readiness invariant
+
+The prior fix preserved an anchor only while `activeElement` remained the video. That was insufficient: WebKit’s native shadow-control Tab movement can scroll backwards before the alignment frame runs. The Film Room controls owner could then disable/remove the focused native control, causing a transient BODY focus state; a subsequent active-element check could no longer recover it. The frozen-HEAD full run’s real failure remains recorded in `/tmp/directors-final-browser.log` and its trace; the strong regression was not changed or skipped.
+
+The repair uses an explicit native-keyboard frame token, acquired only by a keyboard control key while the native player is already ready/focused. Film Room’s existing controls owner honors that token so the control cannot disappear during the brief alignment interval. The hook queues alignment independently of pending hash state and tolerates transient BODY shadow-focus states. Tab, Space and native seek/volume arrows preserve readiness. It does not enable the opening aperture’s player before readiness.
+
+Real external HTMLElement focus clears the hold through a document-level focus listener, covering header/skip controls outside the story as well as downstream story controls. That listener does not scroll. Existing Tab-only story recentering remains scoped to actual Tab focus. Pointer/touch, wheel, page-scroll keys, new anchor navigation and cleanup release the token. Thus external programmatic `preventScroll` focus is preserved, and intentional manual reverse scrolling can disable/hide the native controls again as before.
+
+The original full native-player Tab traversal regression is unchanged. A new focused test covers ArrowRight, Space and ArrowUp readiness, external skip-link `preventScroll` focus without scroll movement, reacquiring readiness without pending hash alignment, then actual wheel/manual-touch emulation releasing the hold and reversing readiness. Pointer/programmatic story-focus regressions remain green.
+
+## 3. Pre-hydration darkness with finite readable fallback
+
+SSR defaults intentionally showed the static composition for no-JS/reduced-motion, but on an enabled-JS connection those defaults could paint before the motion hook initialized. A homepage-only head bootstrap now marks normal-motion loading before body paint. Scoped CSS hides the cinematic layers/statement/details while that guard is pending; the header and persistent accessible H1 stay available.
+
+The hero reports readiness only after its timeline, initial dark styles and pin handlers are established. A five-second watchdog releases the guard into the readable static composition if enhancement never arrives. Late enhancement after that fallback does not abruptly hide the already-readable frame. A caught initialization failure reverts partial GSAP styles and also selects the fallback. Reduced motion does not activate the guard; no-JS never executes it. This adds no waiting/loading screen for those modes, and leaves a finite readable fallback when chunks fail.
+
+The delayed-JavaScript test pauses each JS response by 1800ms and takes an immediate computed-style snapshot after 1000ms from navigation commit. It does not retry the initial opacity assertion until hydration. Against the old build it observed statement opacity **1** instead of **0**, reproducing the review. Against the rebuilt candidate it observes every cinematic layer/statement at opacity 0 and no pin at that moment, then verifies normal enhancement becomes ready. A separate blocked-JS test observes the finite timeout and readable fallback; no-JS/reduced-motion tests verify the static frame without a pin. A source fixture deliberately throws during GSAP timeline creation and verifies style reversion/readable fallback without a page error.
+
+## Actual commands and results
+
+Managed Chromium is the default throughout. WebKit uses the existing host libraries with `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu`; this skips host dependency preflight only, not browser cases/assertions.
+
+- Before repairs: `npx playwright test tests/e2e/arrival-loading.spec.ts tests/e2e/arrival-directors-cut.spec.ts --grep 'slow enabled|rear and white' --project=desktop-chromium --workers=1 --output=/tmp/directors-final-wave-red`: two failures. The frame failure was the intended 1440px bound failure. The first draft loading test retried opacity into hydration and failed on absent readiness instead; corrected it to a true immediate initial-paint snapshot.
+- Corrected initial-paint RED: `npx playwright test tests/e2e/arrival-loading.spec.ts --grep 'slow enabled' --project=desktop-chromium --workers=1 --output=/tmp/directors-final-initial-red`: one expected failure, 1000ms statement opacity 1.
+- Source fixtures after repair: `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu npx playwright test tests/e2e/arrival-directors-cut.spec.ts --grep 'rear and white|failed hero enhancement' --project=desktop-chromium --project=iphone-webkit --workers=1 --output=/tmp/directors-final-source-green`: **4/4 passed**.
+- Preserved native WebKit regression under concurrent worker load: `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu npx playwright test tests/e2e/cinema-framing.spec.ts --grep 'direct film landing lets' --project=iphone-webkit --repeat-each=8 --workers=2 --output=/tmp/directors-final-native-repeat`: **8/8 passed in 26.4s**. Log `/tmp/directors-final-native-repeat.log`. Ran concurrently with the covering suite below; not a physical-device or thermal measurement.
+- Built loading/fallback/native release/story-focus coverage: `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu npx playwright test tests/e2e/arrival-loading.spec.ts tests/e2e/cinema-framing.spec.ts tests/e2e/story-focus.spec.ts --grep 'slow enabled|failed JavaScript|no-js keeps|reduced keeps|ready native keyboard|pointer focus|programmatic preventScroll|real Tab' --project=desktop-chromium --project=iphone-webkit --workers=1 --output=/tmp/directors-final-loading-controls`: **16/16 passed in 34.0s**. Log `/tmp/directors-final-loading-controls.log`.
+- Visual capture invocation: `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_VALIDATE_HOST_REQUIREMENTS=1 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/zk17-libs/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu node --input-type=module < /tmp/directors-final-wave-visual.mjs`: exit 0, eight actual built desktop/WebKit midpoint/hold screenshots, with settled photo opacity >.95. No fixture timeline forcing; scroll positions are set programmatically and the real DOM is observed. Log `/tmp/directors-final-wave-visual.log`.
+- Scoped ESLint on changed layout/components/browser tests passed. `npm run typecheck` passed after the core product patch; the controller subsequently reported current full lint/typecheck and 56 unit tests passing after the external-focus follow-up. An initial ESLint ref-immutability finding on `element.dataset` assignment was corrected to DOM `setAttribute` calls before the clean scoped run. No rule was disabled.
+- `git diff --check` on owned files passed before commit.
+
+## Controller gates and limits
+
+All product/test source was frozen after the covering passes. The controller has started a new full browser run at `/tmp/directors-release-browser.log`; this report does not predeclare its result. The earlier twelve-case canonical capture passed for `f72f858`, but must be regenerated for these visual/initial-load fixes. Canonical capture/metrics regeneration, asset-hash final checks, scoped independent re-review and preview review remain controller-owned.
+
+Touch-enabled WebKit captures/programmatic scrolling do not prove physical iOS swipes, Safari toolbar behavior, authored playback pacing, hardware/GPU smoothness or thermal performance. The new video regression repetitions establish correctness in the managed browser under the reported test concurrency, not a device-performance guarantee. Existing preview-only media rights/source limitations remain unchanged and block a production-readiness claim; no release safeguard was bypassed.
